      "page_title": "Awaitables: Coroutines, Tasks, Futures",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What is an awaitable?",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "==Awaitable== = any object you can put after `await`",
            "Kinds: **coroutine**, **Task**, **Future**",
            "Or any object whose `__await__` method returns an __iterator__"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A ==coroutine== runs only when it is awaited",
            "It runs **sequentially**, inside its caller",
            "Awaiting it creates __no__ concurrency by itself"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Future = result slot",
          "items": [
            "Low-level placeholder for a result that arrives __later__",
            "States: {blue:pending} → {green:done} / {red:cancelled}",
            "`set_result()`, `set_exception()`",
            "`add_done_callback(cb)` runs cb when done"
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A ==Task== is a __Future subclass__ that wraps a coroutine and schedules it on the loop **immediately**. It is the unit of concurrency.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Coroutine vs Task vs Future",
          "ink": "purple"
